# DFL GameObject Lifecycle

All runtime GameObjects are owned by the container scope. Never create or destroy them with raw Unity APIs in game code.

## Prohibited

```csharp
// BAD -- bypasses pooling, asset loading, and scope teardown
var go = new GameObject("Fx");
var instance = Object.Instantiate(prefab);
Object.Destroy(instance);
```

## Required: GameObjectManager Checkout

Register prefabs on the builder with an **explicit warm policy**, inject the manager, and `Checkout` with a `CancellationToken` from the owning scope or controller. The framework check-ins (or destroys) the instance when that token is cancelled — typically when the child container / controller scope disposes.

```csharp
// In ContainerBuilderFactory / Factory — pick a warm policy deliberately:
builder.AddGameObjectManager(_shopCardPrefab, PoolWarmConfig.BuildRequired(16, 16).ToOptions());
// or Background / OnDemand via PoolWarmConfig — see dfl-asset-loading

// In service / controller:
[Dependency] private readonly IGameObjectManagerT<ShopCardView> _cards = default!;
[Dependency] private readonly CancellationToken _token = default!;

ShopCardView card = await _cards.Checkout(parent, token);
// no Release/Destroy — token cancellation returns it to the pool
```

Do **not** default every registration to `GameObjectManagerOptions.OnDemand`. Cold first `Checkout` Instantiates on the main thread and causes sheet/UI hitches. Use `BuildRequired` (or `Background`) with a catalog-sized `prewarmCount` for UI the player will open in-session.

Prefer MVC `Factory` for UI/features (the framework checkouts the view for you). Use `IGameObjectManager` / `IGameObjectManagerT<T>` for pooled objects outside MVC (FX, labels, projectiles).

## Scene-placed objects

Roots that live in a loaded scene are registered with `AddScene<T>`, not spawned via managers. They unload when the scene's container disposes.
